\title{The Fractal Dimension of Mathematical Truth:\\Prefix Geometry, Symbolic Counting, and Computability}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained model connecting binary truth assignments, prefix geometry, symbolic fractal dimension, real-number coding, and computability. An infinite theory is represented by a stream $x\in\{0,1\}^{\N}$. The weighted disagreement function
\[
d(x,y)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\ind_{x_n\ne y_n}2^{-(n+1)}
\]
is a metric. We study a paired truth language in which every even coordinate is fixed to $1$ and every odd coordinate is free. Its admissible prefixes of length $2n$ number $2^n$, whereas the ambient count is $2^{2n}$. The exact identity $(2^n)^2=2^{2n}$ yields symbolic prefix-counting dimension $1/2$, strictly between $0$ and $1$. Every stream determines a binary real $R(x)=\sum_{n\ge0}x_n2^{-(n+1)}$; its first $N$ terms approximate it from below with error at most $2^{-N}$, and agreement on the first $N$ bits implies real-value distance at most $2^{-N}$. Finally, halting truth for programs on a fixed input is undecidable but recursively enumerable. The construction rigorously bridges symbolic geometry, analysis, and computability without claiming that all mathematical truth has a canonical dimension or that the elementary half-free model is uncomputable.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

After choosing a language, semantics, foundational setting, and enumeration of statements, a complete binary truth assignment becomes an infinite stream. This representation invites several questions. If two assignments agree for a long initial segment, should they count as close? If a family permits relatively few prefixes, can its exponential prefix growth define a dimension? If its bits are binary digits of a real, how accurately do finite observations determine that real? What changes when the bits encode an undecidable predicate such as program halting?

These questions must be separated. There is no canonical effective enumeration of all mathematical statements independent of syntax and foundations. Dimension depends on a metric or covering convention. Geometric approximation of a real does not imply computability of its bits, and undecidability of one truth predicate does not transfer to an unrelated language.

We construct an explicit model in which every connection has a precise statement. The paired truth language fixes one coordinate in each pair and leaves the other free. It has exact finite-scale prefix counts and symbolic dimension $1/2$. Independently, a binary-real map supplies certified approximations and a prefix continuity bound. Finally, halting truth is shown to be noncomputable as a decision predicate but recursively enumerable in the positive direction. The construction is a model, not a claim about a coding-independent invariant of mathematics.

\section{Binary theories and prefix geometry}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{binary theory} is a function $x:\N\to\{0,1\}$. The value $x_n=1$ means that the $n$th statement is accepted as true. No deductive-closure condition is imposed.
\end{definition}

For a proposition $Q$, let $\ind_Q$ be $1$ when $Q$ holds and $0$ otherwise.

\begin{definition}
The weighted disagreement distance is
\[
d(x,y)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\ind_{x_n\ne y_n}2^{-(n+1)}.
\]
\end{definition}
The series converges because every summand is bounded by $2^{-(n+1)}$ and the sum of these bounds is $1$.

\begin{theorem}[Prefix Metric Theorem]
For all binary theories $x,y,z$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $d(x,y)\ge0$;
\item $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$;
\item $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$;
\item $d(x,z)\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$.
\end{enumerate}
Thus $d$ is a metric on $\{0,1\}^{\N}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Nonnegativity and symmetry hold termwise. If $x=y$, all indicators vanish. If $x\ne y$, some coordinate $m$ differs and contributes the positive amount $2^{-(m+1)}$, so the distance is positive. At each coordinate,
\[
\ind_{x_n\ne z_n}\le \ind_{x_n\ne y_n}+\ind_{y_n\ne z_n},
\]
because an intermediate bit cannot equal two unequal endpoint bits. Multiply by $2^{-(n+1)}$ and sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Prefix bound]
For all $x,y$, one has $0\le d(x,y)\le1$. If $x_n=y_n$ for every $n<N$, then
\[
d(x,y)\le2^{-N}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Replace each indicator by $1$ for the global upper bound. Under prefix agreement the first $N$ terms vanish, leaving
\[
\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}2^{-(n+1)}=2^{-N}.
\]
\end{proof}

\section{The paired truth language}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{paired truth language} $\Pcal$ is
\[
\Pcal=\{x\in\{0,1\}^{\N}:x_{2k}=1\text{ for every }k\in\N\}.
\]
A finite paired description of scale $n$ is a word $p=(p_0,\ldots,p_{n-1})\in\{0,1\}^n$ storing the first $n$ odd-coordinate choices.
\end{definition}

Given $p$, define a completed stream $C_p$ by $(C_p)_{2k+1}=p_k$ for $0\le k<n$ and by setting every other coordinate to $1$.

\begin{lemma}[Completion Lemma]
Every finite paired description extends to a stream in $\Pcal$. The completion recovers every stored bit, and $p\mapsto C_p$ is injective.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
All even coordinates of $C_p$ are $1$, while coordinate $2k+1$ returns $p_k$. If $p\ne q$, some stored coordinate differs, and the corresponding completed streams differ at its odd position.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Prefix Counting Theorem]
For every $n\ge0$, the number $A_n$ of paired prefixes of length $2n$ is $2^n$. The number $B_m$ of unrestricted binary prefixes of length $m$ is $2^m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A paired prefix has $n$ prescribed even positions and $n$ independently chosen odd positions, giving $A_n=2^n$. An unrestricted length-$m$ prefix has two choices at each position, giving $B_m=2^m$.
\end{proof}

The first six scales are:
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrrr}
n&0&1&2&3&4&5\\ \hline
A_n&1&2&4&8&16&32\\
B_{2n}&1&4&16&64&256&1024
\end{array}
\]
In each column, $A_n^2=B_{2n}$.

\section{Exact symbolic dimension}

\begin{definition}
For $L\subseteq\{0,1\}^{\N}$, let $N_L(m)$ be the number of length-$m$ prefixes occurring in $L$. When the limit exists, its normalized symbolic prefix-counting dimension is
\[
\dim_{\rm sym}(L)=\lim_{m\to\infty}\frac{\log N_L(m)}{m\log2}.
\]
\end{definition}

The denominator is the logarithm of the ambient count $2^m$. Thus bounded prefix growth has dimension $0$, while the full binary space has dimension $1$.

\begin{theorem}[Exact Half-Dimension Theorem]
At every even scale $2n$,
\[
N_{\Pcal}(2n)^2=2^{2n}=N_{\rm full}(2n).
\]
Consequently,
\[
\dim_{\rm sym}(\Pcal)=\frac12,
\qquad 0<\frac12<1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The counting theorem gives $N_{\Pcal}(2n)=2^n$, hence its square is $2^{2n}$. At even scales the normalized logarithm is exactly
\[
\frac{\log 2^n}{(2n)\log2}=\frac12.
\]
At an odd scale $2n+1$, there remain exactly $n$ free odd positions, so $N_{\Pcal}(2n+1)=2^n$ and the ratio $n/(2n+1)$ tends to $1/2$. Therefore the full limit exists.
\end{proof}

The density of admissible words at even scale is $2^n/2^{2n}=2^{-n}$, which tends exponentially to zero. Nevertheless, the prefix count itself grows exponentially. Dimension detects the positive rate of retained freedom despite vanishing density. The result is a statement about symbolic prefix-counting dimension. Relating it to a chosen Hausdorff or box-counting dimension requires a cylinder-cover theory and fixed metric normalization.

\section{Binary real coding}

\begin{definition}
For a binary theory $x$, define
\[
R(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}x_n2^{-(n+1)},
\qquad
R_N(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x_n2^{-(n+1)}.
\]
The first quantity lies in $[0,1]$, and the second is its $N$-term dyadic truncation.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Binary Approximation Theorem]
For every $x$ and $N\ge0$,
\[
0\le R(x)-R_N(x)\le2^{-N}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The difference is the nonnegative tail
\[
\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}x_n2^{-(n+1)}.
\]
Replacing each $x_n$ by $1$ bounds it by the geometric series $\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}2^{-(n+1)}=2^{-N}$. The estimate is sharp when every omitted bit is $1$.
\end{proof}

Thus the first $N$ bits certify that
\[
R(x)\in[R_N(x),R_N(x)+2^{-N}].
\]
To obtain uncertainty at most $\varepsilon>0$, it is sufficient to take
\[
N\ge\left\lceil\log_2\frac1\varepsilon\right\rceil.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Prefix Stability Theorem]
If $x_n=y_n$ for every $n<N$, then
\[
|R(x)-R(y)|\le2^{-N}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The common prefix cancels:
\[
R(x)-R(y)=\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}(x_n-y_n)2^{-(n+1)}.
\]
Since $|x_n-y_n|\le1$, the triangle inequality bounds the absolute value by the geometric tail $2^{-N}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Lipschitz comparison]
For every $x,y$,
\[
|R(x)-R(y)|\le d(x,y).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At each coordinate $|x_n-y_n|=\ind_{x_n\ne y_n}$. Expand the difference of the series and apply the triangle inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Binary ambiguity]
The map $R$ is not injective on all streams: a terminating binary expansion can equal an expansion ending in infinitely many ones. The prefix metric remains separating, while $R$ identifies these dyadic endpoint pairs. Neither approximation theorem requires injectivity.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

To enumerate the paired prefixes at scale $n$, iterate through the $2^n$ binary words $(b_0,\ldots,b_{n-1})$ and output
\[
(1,b_0,1,b_1,\ldots,1,b_{n-1}).
\]
This costs $O(n2^n)$ time, which is optimal up to constants for writing all outputs, and $O(n)$ working space if outputs are streamed.

For certified approximation, read the first $N$ bits, form the exact dyadic rational $R_N(x)$, and return $[R_N(x),R_N(x)+2^{-N}]$. The procedure takes $O(N)$ arithmetic steps and an $O(N)$-bit numerator. To compare two streams with a promised common prefix, inspect the first $N$ positions in $O(N)$ time and return the bound $2^{-N}$.

These procedures respectively demonstrate exact combinatorial growth, geometric convergence, and numerical stability under shared information.

\section{Computability and halting truth}

A predicate on program codes is \emph{decidable} if an algorithm halts on every code and gives the correct answer. It is \emph{recursively enumerable} if every positive case is eventually confirmed, although negative cases may run forever.

Fix an input $u$. For a program code $c$, define
\[
H_u(c)=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\text{program $c$ eventually halts on input $u$}.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Halting Truth Theorem]
For every fixed input $u$, the predicate $H_u$ is not computable, but its positive instances are recursively enumerable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If a total decider for $H_u$ existed, program specialization could encode an arbitrary program-input pair as a program acting on the fixed input $u$. The assumed decider would solve the general halting problem, contradicting the diagonal halting theorem.

For recursive enumerability, simulate programs on $u$. A semidecision procedure accepts when the selected program halts. To enumerate all positives, dovetail: at stage $t$, simulate each of the first $t$ programs for $t$ steps and announce newly observed halts. Every halting computation has finite duration and is eventually discovered.
\end{proof}

The Binary Approximation Theorem applies to every stream, including a stream made from halting bits after choosing an enumeration. But it assumes access to the first $N$ bits; it does not decide them. Analytic control of a series tail and effective availability of digits are distinct.

Chaitin's halting probability $\Omega$ is defined for a prefix-free machine by summing $2^{-|p|}$ over halting programs $p$. Prefix-freeness and Kraft's inequality bound the sum. The coordinate-weighted coding $R(x)$ resembles this construction but is not automatically an $\Omega$ number. No prefix-free machine is part of the paired language, whose dimension $1/2$ is elementary and computable. Thus neither geometric convergence nor halting undecidability implies that the paired dimension is uncomputable.

\section{Applications and discussion}

The paired language is a periodic symbolic constraint with one free bit in each two-bit block. Its dimension is also a normalized information rate: $n$ independent bits survive in a word of length $2n$. More generally, a periodic block of length $b$ with $a$ free positions should have $2^{an}$ admissible words at scale $bn$ and symbolic dimension $a/b$.

Prefix metrics also occur in tries, digital trees, coding theory, hierarchical classification, and streaming computation. The estimate $2^{-N}$ converts shared initial information into a certified uncertainty radius. Each additional bit halves the worst-case interval for the coded real.

Halting truth illustrates one-sided knowledge: positive facts can emerge over time even when no terminating decision procedure handles all cases. This distinction underlies computably enumerable theories and monotone approximations to halting-probability reals. It also shows why a simple finite error bound need not make the digits producing a real effectively accessible.

\section{Limitations and future work}

The phrase ``the fractal dimension of mathematical truth'' becomes definite only after choosing a syntax, semantics, foundational theory, enumeration, metric, and dimension notion. Altering these choices may alter the geometry. The paired language is an explicit toy language, not the set of all true mathematical sentences, and its theorem concerns symbolic prefix-counting dimension.

Future work may establish that the prefix metric induces the product topology on Cantor space and investigate completeness and compactness. Cylinder covers can support upper and lower box-counting dimensions and connect exact prefix counts to geometric dimensions. A prefix-free machine can be introduced to derive Kraft boundedness, monotone rational approximations to its halting probability, and the recovery of finite halting information from sufficiently precise approximations.

A deeper direction is effective Hausdorff dimension of individual streams through asymptotic prefix-free Kolmogorov complexity, the standard setting where fractal dimension, algorithmic randomness, and halting-probability reals meet. The paired model should be generalized to periodic masks with $a$ free coordinates in blocks of length $b$. Finally, bi-Lipschitz coding-invariance theorems would clarify which dimensions survive changes of statement enumeration.

\section{Conclusion}

An infinite binary truth assignment carries a natural prefix-sensitive metric. Fixing one bit in every pair leaves $2^n$ admissible prefixes at length $2n$, against $2^{2n}$ ambient prefixes. Their exact square identity yields symbolic dimension $1/2$: vanishing density coexists with positive exponential complexity.

The same streams define binary reals whose first $N$ digits give lower approximations with error at most $2^{-N}$. Common prefixes guarantee numerical stability. Computability enters separately: halting truth is undecidable but recursively enumerable. Symbolic counting measures retained freedom, prefix geometry measures informational agreement, binary series provide analytic error bounds, and computability distinguishes approximation from decision. Together these results form a precise model for the geometry of truth while explaining why no coding-independent dimension of all mathematical truth has been established.

\end{document}

